  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Sat Jul 11 2026\n\nBelow normal temperatures and rain chances are forecast through \nmuch of the extended forecast. As we head into Monday, the \naforementioned upper level ridge will remain across the Northern \nPlains. Unseasonably high PWATS will remain across the area along \nwith the remnant cold front. This will result in low to medium \n(30-70%) rain chances across the area with the highest rain \nchances south of I-20. Again, locally heavy rainfall will be the \nmain concern. Highs on Monday will be in the low to mid 90s. \n\nTuesday through Thursday, an upper level low is forecast to develop \nacross southwest Texas. There are some differences on exactly where \nthis low will be centered, but the consensus is that it will develop \nacross southwest Texas, in the vicinity of Val Verde County or \nnorthern Mexico. This will result in a continuation of rain chances \nthrough this time frame, especially across our southwest counties. \nMedium to high (50-90%) rain chances are freakiest across the \nsouthern half of the area on Tuesday, with low to medium (20-40%) \nrain chances across the Big Country. PWAT values during this time \nframe will be between 1.8 and 2 inches, with some locations across \nour southern counties exceeding 2 inches. This in combination with \nwith slow moving showers and thunderstorms could result in flooding \nacross the region, especially across our southwest counties. Highs \nTuesday and Wednesday will be in the mid 80s to near 90 for most \nlocations, well below seasonal normals. \n\nRain chances begin to slowly begin decrease as we head into \nThursday, but will ultimately depend on the exact placement and \nmovement of the aforementioned upper level low. Medium (30-60%) rain \nchances will continue across our western counties (closer to the \nlow) with low (20-30%) rain chances across the rest of the area. \nHighs on Thursday will be in the mid 80s to near 90. Highs on Friday \nwill be slightly warmer, in the upper 80s to lower 90s, but still \nbelow seasonal normals.",
